1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,280
Aziz sends money to his mother from his phone, and the bank asks for no face check.

2
00:00:05,330 --> 00:00:07,872
Yet the bank is sure that it is really Aziz.

3
00:00:07,922 --> 00:00:14,624
So how can a bank know it is really Aziz, without a face check every time?

4
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:20,672
The answer is heeyID, a customer identity platform that the bank runs inside its own perimeter.

5
00:00:20,722 --> 00:00:28,160
This course follows Aziz on one journey, from his first code to his first transfer, and the map of the platform appears on the way.

6
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:31,376
On a Sunday, Aziz installs the mobile bank.

7
00:00:31,426 --> 00:00:34,808
It is the master app of the bank: the app that moves money.

8
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:38,744
He types his phone number, and an SMS code arrives.

9
00:00:38,794 --> 00:00:44,096
TAF sends it: the state antifraud service of the mobile operators.

10
00:00:44,146 --> 00:00:45,968
He types the code.

11
00:00:46,018 --> 00:00:51,560
TAF checks that the code arrived on this phone, with this SIM card, and its verdict is green.

12
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:54,776
Then he sets a password and types it a second time.

13
00:00:54,826 --> 00:00:57,296
Now Aziz has an account at level one.

14
00:00:57,346 --> 00:01:00,968
The bank knows his phone number, but it does not yet know the person.

15
00:01:01,018 --> 00:01:04,616
The next Saturday, Aziz wants to send money to his mother.

16
00:01:04,666 --> 00:01:09,584
Money needs level two: the bank must know the real person, with a real passport.

17
00:01:09,634 --> 00:01:13,808
First, the app asks for his consent, and the platform records it.

18
00:01:13,858 --> 00:01:19,352
Then MyID, the national identity service, opens inside the mobile bank.

19
00:01:19,402 --> 00:01:23,312
It compares his live face and his passport with the state records.

20
00:01:23,362 --> 00:01:26,480
Next, the platform checks that the phone number is his.

21
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:29,168
It looks first at the number on his MyID record.

22
00:01:29,218 --> 00:01:34,856
When MyID has no number, or a different one, TAF says who owns the number.

23
00:01:34,906 --> 00:01:37,088
Now Aziz is at level two.

24
00:01:37,138 --> 00:01:41,600
And his phone becomes the trusted phone: the one phone that moves his money.

25
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:43,616
The transfer to his mother goes.

26
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:48,944
The next day, Aziz opens the mobile bank again, and there is no face check.

27
00:01:48,994 --> 00:01:53,936
When he logs out, he logs in again on the same phone, with his password and a code.

28
00:01:53,986 --> 00:01:55,808
Now the worry.

29
00:01:55,858 --> 00:02:00,296
A stranger talks the mobile operator into a new SIM card with Aziz's number.

30
00:02:00,346 --> 00:02:04,400
He also has Aziz's password, from a leak on another website.

31
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:08,024
He puts the SIM card in his own phone and installs the mobile bank.

32
00:02:08,074 --> 00:02:12,248
He has the number and the password, but he does not have the trusted phone.

33
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:19,376
A phone becomes trusted only when Aziz's own face passes MyID on it, and TAF confirms its SIM card.

34
00:02:19,426 --> 00:02:22,568
The stranger cannot bring Aziz's face.

35
00:02:22,618 --> 00:02:25,136
So his phone never moves Aziz's money.

36
00:02:25,186 --> 00:02:31,304
Only the trusted phone moves money, and only the face of the real person makes a phone trusted.

37
00:02:31,354 --> 00:02:33,680
Now look at the map behind that journey.

38
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:40,040
The mobile bank calls the heeyID API, and every request enters through one edge.

39
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:42,632
The edge sends it to the customer gateway.

40
00:02:42,682 --> 00:02:49,736
All the rules of the bank live in that gateway: levels, limits, devices, sessions and consent.

41
00:02:49,786 --> 00:02:55,832
Next to the gateway is Keycloak, and it does one job: it issues the tokens that the app carries.

42
00:02:55,882 --> 00:02:58,664
The bank's staff work in the administrator panel.

43
00:02:58,714 --> 00:03:03,632
It talks to the admin gateway, which uses the same rules, not a second copy.

44
00:03:03,682 --> 00:03:06,200
Under the gateways are four stores.

45
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:08,072
Postgres keeps the records.

46
00:03:08,122 --> 00:03:11,240
Valkey keeps the counters and the cool-downs.

47
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:13,064
Kafka carries the events out.

48
00:03:13,114 --> 00:03:14,936
Vault keeps the keys.

49
00:03:14,986 --> 00:03:18,272
The gateway never calls the outside world directly.

50
00:03:18,322 --> 00:03:26,072
It uses adapters, one for each service: MyID, TAF, SMS and push.

51
00:03:26,122 --> 00:03:31,544
Only the customer gateway and the workers, which run the background jobs, call the adapters.

52
00:03:31,594 --> 00:03:35,840
Now draw one frame around all of it: the bank's perimeter.

53
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:38,240
One installation serves one bank.

54
00:03:38,290 --> 00:03:44,864
The TAF agent runs inside, in the bank's network, and the TAF adapter calls it.

55
00:03:44,914 --> 00:03:50,336
Three services are outside: MyID, the SMS vendor and the push service.

56
00:03:50,386 --> 00:03:52,808
The push service gets no personal data.

57
00:03:52,858 --> 00:03:58,472
Aziz proves who he is one time, and his trusted phone carries that proof every day.

58
00:03:58,522 --> 00:04:02,992
So how many times does Aziz meet MyID?

59
00:04:03,042 --> 00:04:04,864
Once.

60
00:04:04,914 --> 00:04:11,752
Lesson 2 gives the words behind this journey: authentication, identity proofing and authorisation.
